<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="34945" href="schedule/2004/fall/PHYS/598/34945.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2004" href="schedule/2004.xml">2004</calendarYear><term id="120048" href="schedule/2004/fall.xml">Fall 2004</term><subject id="PHYS" href="schedule/2004/fall/PHYS.xml">Physics</subject><course id="598" href="schedule/2004/fall/PHYS/598.xml">Special Topics in Physics</course></parents><sectionNumber>ESM</sectionNumber><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>EMERGENT STATES OF MATTER.  PHYS 598ESM is an introduction to the consequences of broken symmetry in condensed matter, the emergence of novel ground states, and the nature of the excitations that arise. Specific systems covered include superconductivity, superfluidity, Bose-Einstein condensates, the quantum hall states and liquid crystals. Prerequisites:  PHYS 504 (PHYCS 462) and PHYS 580 (PHYCS 480), or consent of instructor.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2004-08-25Z</startDate><endDate>2004-12-10Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="L4 ">Lecture</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>10:20 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>158</roomNumber><buildingName>Loomis Laboratory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Goldenfeld" firstName="N">Goldenfeld, N</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>